        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Warranty Guide", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-warranty-guide/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What does a treadmill warranty cover?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Defects in materials and workmanship, usually split into separate terms for the frame, the drive motor, parts such as electronics and rollers, and labour. It typically does not cover wear items like the belt and deck beyond a limited period, damage from misuse or missing maintenance, commercial use of a home machine, or use outside the environment the manual specifies.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What does a lifetime warranty on a treadmill mean?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually that a specific component — often the frame or the drive motor — is covered for as long as the original purchaser owns the machine, in residential use. It rarely includes labour, often excludes related parts such as the motor controller, and may depend on registration. Selling the machine typically ends it. The warranty document defines it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is an extended warranty on a treadmill worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Generally not on a budget machine, where the cost is a large share of the price. On a mid-range machine it is arguable, depending on whether service is in-home, whether wear items are excluded and whether a maintenance record is required. On premium machines with long factory terms it is often unnecessary. Compare it line by line with the factory warranty.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What voids a treadmill warranty?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Commonly: commercial use of a residential machine, exceeding the user weight limit, the wrong lubricant or no maintenance, use in an environment the manual excludes such as a damp or unheated garage, extension cords or prohibited outlets, incorrect assembly, modifications and unauthorised repairs. Most warranties also end when the machine is sold.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a treadmill warranty cover labor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually for a limited period — often one year on home machines, sometimes more on premium models — and that is the term to read first. After it expires, covered parts may still be supplied free, but you fit them yourself or pay a technician. Check whether labour is in-home or return-to-base, since shipping a treadmill is rarely practical.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a treadmill warranty transferable?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually not. Most home treadmill warranties apply only to the original purchaser, so a used treadmill normally comes without one. A few manufacturers allow transfers, sometimes for a fee or with registration. If you are buying used, assume there is no warranty unless the manufacturer confirms otherwise in writing.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
